  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nUpper ridging will begin to push eastward Monday with the ridge axis \nbeing well east of the FA by Monday afternoon. An upper low will \npush southward along the West Coast during this time before pushing \neastward early Tuesday. This will set the stage for an increasingly \nmore likely heavy rain event Tuesday into Wednesday. The upper low \nwill tap both Gulf moisture and deep, rich moisture from both \ntropical systems in the eastern Pacific. The first round of heavy \nrainfall will occur across the Four Corners region late Monday with \nchances increasing eastward Tuesday as the upper low begins moving \nacross the Desert Southwest. Rain chances, at least potential for \nheavy rainfall, will eventually move east of the FA by Thursday as \nthe upper low begins to move over the Southern Plains. Some wrap-\naround showers are possible Thursday afternoon and evening, but this \nwill be generally light. A potential twist in the forecast is the \npossibility for a cold front to move into the FA ahead of the upper \nlow as the GFS continues to show. While the front progged by the GFS \nis much weaker than it has been in recent runs, it would still be \nenough to provide enhanced lift to what will already be excellent \nupper level lift and will further increase the threat for heavy \nrainfall. There is still uncertainty in regards to timing of \nrainfall in addition to the actual path and forward speed of the \nupper low. This will significantly impact where the heaviest \nrainfall will occur. Regardless, this system bares watching with \nhydrological highlights being needed over the next few days.",
